WASHINGTON—U.S. Senator John Boozman (R-AR) and Ranking Member of the Senate Veterans’ Affairs Committee Richard Blumenthal (D-CT) recently introduced the Veterans Crisis Line Support and Safety Act, bipartisan legislation that would improve mental health support and workplace conditions for Veterans Crisis Line (VCL) employees, many of whom are veterans themselves.

“The men and women providing instant and critical mental health support to veterans during some of their most difficult moments are an invaluable, life-saving resource. Many VCL employees are veterans themselves and understand the challenges those they serve are facing. I am proud this bipartisan legislation would guarantee their access to key tools and protections for their wellbeing, ensuring assistance for veterans in crisis is not delayed or denied,” said Boozman, a senior member of the Veterans’ Affairs Committee and Chairman of the Military Construction and VA Appropriations Subcommittee.

“Veterans Crisis Line employees do lifesaving work every day in the most difficult circumstances. Unfortunately, these workers often face emotionally challenging situations. Our bipartisan bill will provide these employees much-needed mental health services and increased workplace protections to ensure they are given the support and space they need to serve veterans,” said Blumenthal.

The VCL provides a life-saving service for responding to veterans in crisis and is an important tool for preventing veteran suicide—offering 24/7 confidential assistance through call, text or chat support. The VCL Support and Safety Act was drafted with input from employees who provided testimony about being harassed in their jobs and needing additional workplace protections given the sensitive and emotional hardships of their work. Full impact statements from VCL responders can be found HERE, HERE and HERE.

Among its many provisions, the Veterans Crisis Line Support and Safety Act would:

Offer VCL employees free, confidential counseling and mental health care from licensed providers experienced in PTSD, trauma and compassion fatigue;

Establish peer support programs, critical incident stress debriefing and annual resiliency/burnout-prevention training;

Provide critical mental health services during and outside of scheduled work hours through VA and outside providers;

Implement reasonable limits on VCL employees’ workload and shift duration;

Require mandatory rest and recovery periods after high-risk or traumatic interactions;

Designate dedicated staff for complex or disruptive cases; and

Ensure staffing levels at the VCL are informed by data on the volume of calls and workforce assessments.

The Veterans Crisis Line Support and Safety Act is endorsed by the American Federation of Government Employees (AFGE) National Veterans Affairs Council.

WASHINGTON – Yesterday, Senator Ruben Gallego (D-AZ) joined Senators John Hickenlooper (D-CO), Martin Heinrich (D-NM), Jeff Merkley (D-OR), Alex Padilla (D-CA), Jacky Rosen (D-NV), Ben Ray Luján (D-NM), Catherine Cortez Masto (D-NV), Chris Van Hollen (D-MD), and Dick Durbin (D-IL) in sending a letter urging the U.S. Department of Agriculture Under Secretary Michael Boren and U.S. Forest Service Chief Tom Schultz, to maintain consistent environmental protections and local input mechanisms in any attempted revision of the 2005 Travel Management Rule.

“For years, the current rule has successfully balanced the use of motorized vehicles with other uses on public lands — ensuring that motorized use in our forests is managed responsibly, centering forest-level decision-making with state and local input, and protecting the environment,” wrote the senators. “Many of the proposed changes in the U.S. Department of Agriculture’s recently published Notice of Intent (NOI) would significantly alter the overarching framework that protects our lands and waters and supports our nation’s wildlife, clean water, and outdoor recreation economy.”

The letter identifies six core principles that the administration should maintain in any revisions to the 2005 Travel Management Rule:

Provide Meaningful Guidance that Helps Avoid Environmental Impacts;

Center State, Tribal, and Local Input;

Consider Impacts to All Types of Recreation and Uses;

Ensure Adequate Resources for Maintaining all Open Trails and Roads;

Continue to Provide Clear Information; and

Maintain Flexibility that Incorporates Public Input

“We urge the Forest Service to approach these revisions with those principles in mind and to work closely with Congress, Tribal Nations, State and local governments, and the broad range of stakeholders that depend on our national forests. We stand ready to work with you to ensure that any final rule gives land managers the tools to keep our forests accessible, safe, and healthy for generations to come,” concluded the senators.

Senator Gallego has long fought to protect America’s beautiful public lands. Last month, Senator Gallego continued his fight to protect the Roadless Rule, which directly protects America’s national forests. This month the Senate passed the Gallego backed Wildfire Emissions Prevention Act of 2026 which makes it easier for states to preform prescribed burnsthus reducing the risk of wildfires.

Washington, D.C. – U.S. Senators Mazie K. Hirono (D-HI) and Ben Ray Luján (D-NM) introduced the Arts Education for All Act, legislation to support and expand access to arts education and programming for young learners across the country.

“Arts and creative expression are fundamental to a well-rounded education,” said Senator Hirono. “Art education programs provide our students with the unique opportunity to express themselves and expose them to diverse array of topics and disciplines. But these programs are often the first to be cut and the last to be funded, and it’s high time for a fundamental shift in the way we value those courses. I’m proud to sponsor the Arts Education for All Act to expand arts programing to make art more accessible to students across the country.”

The Arts Education for All Act would increase states’ ability to implement arts programming for early learners, K-12 students, and youth and adults involved in the justice system. The legislation would also strengthen research into arts education to help educators and policymakers better understand how the arts can support students and compliment their elementary and secondary education.

“Arts education gives students the opportunity to learn, express themselves, and discover new talents, but too many young people lack access to these opportunities,” said Senator Luján. “All students, no matter where they live or their background, deserve the opportunity to experience the arts. That’s why I’m introducing the Arts Education for All Act to expand access to arts programming and help ensure that the arts remain an important part of educating and supporting the next generation.”

Specifically, the Arts Education for All Act would:

Strengthen the ability to use Child Care and Development Block Grant funding for arts programming;

Expand Elementary and Secondary Education Act (ESEA) plan provisions to include descriptions of how states would support and encourage arts education staff, instruction, and partnerships to improve student achievement across subjects;

Require state report cards under the Every Student Succeeds Act (ESSA) to include information on arts courses and incorporate arts education as an element of K-12 school improvement;

Increase opportunities for local educational agencies to support professional development for arts teachers and the integration of the arts;

Strengthen the integration of arts and arts education through the 21st Century Community Learning Centers program by maximizing partnerships with community and arts-focused organizations to increase arts education and creative youth development in afterschool and summer learning programs;

Allow states to document how they will coordinate juvenile justice and delinquency prevention services and activities with state and local arts agencies and organizations, using arts education to improve reentry efforts and reduce recidivism;

Enhance research activities at the Institute of Education Sciences through a focus on arts and arts education research; and

Direct the National Assessment Governing Board to reinstate arts assessments.

Senator Hirono has long advocated for improving well-rounded public education. This year, she has held 3 spotlight forums highlighting the Trump administration’s attacks on public education and their dangerous impacts on students. Her most recent forum centered on the National Private School Voucher program and its threats to investments in public education. In May, she introduced the All Students Count Act to disaggregate data and ensure Asian American, Native Hawaiian, and Pacific Islander (AANHPI) students are better accounted for by states and school districts and supported by schools across the country. In February, she introduced bicameral legislation to improve public school infrastructure and environments to ensure school facilities are safe, healthy, sustainable, and offer positive learning environments for all students. In 2025, she introduced legislation to promote the teaching of AANHPI history in schools across the country, which would require Presidential and Congressional Academies’ grant applicants and recipients to include AANHPI history as part of their American history and civics programs offered to students and teachers.

Companion legislation was introduced in the U.S. House of Representatives by Representative Suzanne Bonamici (D-OR).

The Arts Education for All Act has been endorsed by 132 national, state, and local organizations and individuals, including Americans for the Arts, the American Art Therapy Association, Carnegie Center for Literacy & Learning, Grantmakers in the Arts, and the National Association for Music Education (NAfME). A full list of endorsements can be found here.

