On Tuesday, U.S. Representative Rick Larsen (WA-02) joined colleagues from the Washington and Oregon congressional delegations in demanding answers and transparency into ongoing efforts by the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) to purchase existing immigration detention centers currently owned and operated by private prison companies like GEO Group and CoreCivic.

“We are deeply concerned about the potential purchase of these facilities, as it appears to be an attempt to circumvent oversight and accountability at detention facilities,” wrote the Members. “Both GEO Group and DHS have stated that acquiring these facilities is aimed, at least in part, at circumventing attempts by states to conduct oversight at immigration detention centers located within their borders.”

The Northwest ICE Processing Center (NWIPC) in Tacoma, Washington is the largest ICE facility in the Pacific Northwest and one of the largest in the nation. ICE has a contract with GEO group to own and operate the facility. In June 2025, Rep. Larsen toured NWIPC and heard from Lelo Juarez, a Skagit County union leader detained by ICE, about poor conditions inside the facility. Larsen and other members of Washington state’s congressional delegation have previously written to ICE to express their concerns about conditions at the facility.

“As of March 2026, the Washington State Department of Health received over 3,500 complaints about Northwest Detention Center. Individuals have reported being sexually assaulted by an officer in their unit; unsanitary conditions, including unclean drinking water, worms, hair, and metal in food, and black mold in showers; and insufficient medical care,” continued the Members. “State oversight is an essential way to help ensure that the people detained in these facilities at U.S. taxpayer expense are being treated with dignity. Attempting to circumvent such oversight by purchasing substandard detention facilities and continuing to allow such inhumane conditions to persist is unacceptable.”

In 2023, the Washington State Legislature passed a law guaranteeing state health inspectors access to privately-owned detention facilities. GEO Group has prevented officials from the Washington State Department of Health from inspecting the facility since 2023, which Washington state is currently challenging in court. ICE’s contract with GEO Group for NWIPC expires on October 27, 2026. If ICE buys NWIPC, it could limit the Washington State Department of Health’s ability to inspect the facility.

The full text of the letter can be found here.

In addition to Rep. Larsen, Representatives Pramila Jayapal (WA-07), Emily Randall (WA-02), Suzanne Bonamici (OR-01), Janelle S. Bynum (OR-05), Suzan DelBene (WA-01), Val Hoyle (OR-04), Maxine Dexter (OR-03), Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (WA-03), Andrea Salinas (OR-06), Kim Schrier (WA-08), Adam Smith (WA-09) and Marilyn Strickland (WA-10) also signed the letter.

Larsen Fights to Hold ICE Accountable

In July 2026, Rep. Larsen conducted an oversight visit of ICE’s Enforcement and Removal Operations (ERO) facility in Ferndale to get answers for the community about the facility’s operations. Last week, Rep. Larsen conducted an oversight visit ofICE’s Homeland Security Investigations (HSI) facility in Ferndale to follow up on questions that he had about HSI’s involvement in ERO operations.

In June 2025, Rep. Larsen conducted an oversight visit of NWIPC and met with Lelo Juarez, a Skagit County union leader and activist whom ICE detained in March 2025.

In Congress, Rep. Larsen has refused to vote for ICE funding without placing additional guardrails on the agency and requiring that ICE follow the same rules as every other local, state and federal law enforcement agency.

Rep. Larsen supports strengthening accountability and oversight of ICE operations and DHS detention facilities, including:

Prohibiting ICE officers from wearing masks during arrests;

Requiring ICE officers to be trained in de-escalation;

Prohibiting masked ICE officers from using restricted equipment, such as tear gas;

Ending family and child detention;

Ending DHS’s use of private detention facilities;

Providing individuals in immigration detention with unlimited communication with legal counsel;

Washington, D.C.– U.S. Representative Derek Tran (CA-45) and members of the California Congressional delegation conducted an unannounced oversight visit to Adelanto ICE Processing Center in Adelanto, California. Alongside Rep. Tran, members of the visiting delegation included Ranking Member of the House Oversight and Government Reform Committee, Representative Robert Garcia (CA-42), and Representatives Judy Chu (CA-28), Dave Min (CA-47), Norma Torres (CA-35), Sydney Kamlager-Dove (CA-37), and Luz Rivas (CA-29). The group toured the facility, where they observed the conditions where detainees are being held. They were not permitted to speak directly with the detained individuals.

“Adelanto has a troubling history of denying detainees proper medical care, allowing unsafe conditions, and refusing to take responsibility for the harrowing deaths that have occurred in its facilities,” said Representative Tran. “We came here to carry out our constitutional responsibilities as Members of Congress: conduct oversight, enforce accountability, and protect our communities. As the son of Vietnamese refugees, this is personal to me — and it’s personal to the hardworking immigrants in my district, too. I will not rest until the Trump Administration answers for the deaths and mistreatment that have taken place at Adelanto, and across the country.”

Federal law guarantees Members of Congress access to Department of Homeland Security (DHS) detention facilities for oversight purposes.

Representative Tran previously visited Adelanto ICE Processing Center in June 2025, where he was denied entry, alongside Representatives Gil Cisneros (CA-31) and Judy Chu (CA-28). Days later, he joined Reps. Cisneros and Chu in a letter to then-Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem and then-Acting ICE Director Todd Lyons condemning the agencies for unlawfully denying them entry to the facility.

Representative Tran introduced the Private Detention and Corporate Accountability Act to hold privately owned detention centers that contract with Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) accountable for wrongful deaths that occur in their facilities or under their care. This legislation would also apply to the Adelanto ICE Processing Center. Tran also co-led the ICE and CBP Constitutional Accountability Act to provide a path to justice for victims of ICE’s constitutional rights abuses.

Tran has voted against H.R. 7147, the Department of Homeland Security Appropriations Act of 2026, which provides more funding for ICE without requiring basic guardrails to keep U.S. citizens from being detained or deported. Tran has also cosponsored the following legislation to curtail ICE’s reckless behavior, increase transparency within ICE-related operations, and affirm the rights of immigrants that are detained by DHS:

H.R. 4176, The No Secret Police Act, to prohibit ICE agents from wearing non-tactical face coverings while on duty;

H.R. 5973, The Stop Excessive Force in Immigration Act, to limit the use of force during immigration operations;

H.R. 3172, The ICE Visibility Act, to require agents to clearly identify themselves as Customs and Border Protection officers when interacting with the public;

H.R. 5653, The Trust Through Transparency Act, to require all ICE officials to wear body cameras when publicly carrying out immigration enforcement operations;

H.R. 944, The Access to Counsel Act, to ensure that U.S. citizens and individuals with lawful status can contact an attorney or family member to seek legal assistance when they are detained;

H.R. 5941, The Restoring Access for Detainees Act, to require DHS to allow noncitizens who have been detained to contact their legal counsel and families; and

H.R. 4456, Stop ICE from Kidnapping U.S. Citizens Act, to prohibit ICE from using federal funds to detain or deport U.S. citizens.

