WASHINGTON, D.C.–U.S. Senator Cory Booker (D-NJ), a member of the Senate Judiciary Committee, introduced the Presidential Pardon Integrity Act, a bill to combat the abuse of the pardon power to reward political allies and campaign donors at the expense of crime victims and American taxpayers. The legislation would deter the corrupt practice of “pardons for sale” by requiring those who buy and sell pardons to disclose their financial ties and contacts with officials, protecting victims’ right to restitution, and insulating the Pardon Attorney from political pressure.

“This President claims to punish the ‘worst of the worst’ yet pardons people convicted of serious crimes because they are his political allies, large campaign donors, or have corporate and financial ties to him and his family. It doesn’t matter whether they have taken responsibility for their offenses. This hypocrisy undermines the integrity of the pardon power and denies victims the justice they deserve,” said Senator Booker. “Pay-for-play pardons that allow political operators to sell pardons to the highest bidder, or the deep-pocketed to write campaign checks, or those willing to commit violence to benefit the President to dodge accountability must be stopped. I am proud to introduce this legislation to combat the abuse of presidential power and ensure the public knows exactly who is benefitting from this corrupt scheme.”

In August, House Democrats on the Committee on the Judiciary released a report exposing MAGA’s cash-for-clemency scheme, under which Trump has sidelined the traditional role of the Office of Pardon Attorney in evaluating clemency petitions on their merits in favor of dispensing pardons to his political supporters, business executives, billionaires, and corrupt politicians. According to a CBS News report, roughly 70% of Mr. Trump’s second-term clemency actions bypassed the traditional DOJ process entirely. Another CBS News report details how, in the absence of a traditional clemency petition review process, a pay-to-play cottage industry has sprung up, populated by former Trump campaign and administration officials who trade on their proximity to the MAGA world and charge clemency petitioners exorbitant fees for access. Under this cash-for-clemency regime, Trump has forgiven an astounding $1.7 billion in fines, forfeitures, and restitution that clemency recipients should have paid back to victims and taxpayers before they were pardoned.

To restore integrity to the pardon process and combat the corrupt practice of “pardons for sale,” the Presidential Pardon Integrity Act would:

Codify the Office of Pardon Attorney to restore objective evaluation of clemency petitions;

Ensure the Pardon Attorney remains a non-political, career position;

Allow crime victims to sue the offenders who harmed them for restitution even after a pardon

Require the Pardon Attorney to disclose to Congress all documentation in the clemency petition process for all clemency grants to increase transparency;

Expose pay-to-play pardons by requiring clemency recipients to disclose whether they provided any benefits to the President, his family, or their businesses, a Cabinet member, or a member of Congress to secure clemency; and

Uncover the pardon broker industry by requiring anyone who was paid to secure clemency for a petitioner to register and report their contacts with Administration officials related to that petition.

WASHINGTON—Last Friday, Congressman James P. McGovern (D-MA), Ranking Member of the House Rules Committee and Co-Chair of the Tom Lantos Human Rights Commission, along with Representative Grace Meng (D-NY) of the House Appropriations Committee’s Subcommittee on National Security, Department of State, and Related Programs, Senator Tim Kaine (D-VA), Ranking Member of the Senate Foreign Relations Subcommittee on Western Hemisphere, Transnational Crime, Civilian Security, Democracy, Human Rights, and Global Women's Issues, and Senator Jeanne Shaheen (D-NH), Ranking Member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, were joined by 42 other Members of Congress in sending a letter urging Secretary of State Marco Rubio to restore policy and programming to counter sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV) globally.

“Congress has recognized SGBV as a national security issue and passed several pertinent bills signed into law by presidents of both parties, including President Trump,” wrote the Members of Congress. “Despite these requirements, the current Trump Administration has defunded programs, terminated staff, and eliminated the structures responsible for preventing and responding to SGBV.”

The letter notes that while Secretary Rubio has testified that he is “not abandoning women’s issues,” the Trump Administration eliminated the State Department’s Secretary’s Office of Global Women’s Issues, failed to integrate USAID’s Gender Equality and Women’s Empowerment office into the State Department, terminated an estimated $400 million in SGBV programs through the closure of USAID, and cancelled “Safe from the Start” award programming.

In the letter, the Members of Congress note that, “In the past year alone, conflict-related sexual violence has been employed as a tactic of war in twenty-one countries, destabilizing governance across whole regions by prompting mass displacement and eroding social cohesion. Lack of accountability for SGBV perpetuates volatility and conflict while inhibiting U.S.-led negotiations and efforts to reach durable peace.”

Specifically, the letter asks Secretary Rubio a series of questions to address:

Status of the U.S. Strategy to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence; and/or plans to spend $187.5 million in Fiscal Year 2026 on such a strategy;

Enumeration of the offices and staff working on preventing and responding to SGBV as part of their job description, and of the meetings with women’s organizations and civil society working to address SGBV;

List of active and eliminated SGBV programs;

Explanation for how the Bureau of Disaster and Humanitarian Response works to integrate SGBV prevention and response into its programming;

Status of the required report on WPS Act Implementation Act.

Other members who signed the letter include Senators Angela D. Alsobrooks (D-MD), Tammy Baldwin (D-WI), Lisa Blunt Rochester (D-DE), Kirsten Gillibrand (D-NY), Mazie K. Hirono (D-HI), Jeffrey A. Merkley (D-OR), Jacky Rosen (D-NV), Adam B. Schiff (D-CA), Raphael Warnock (D-GA), and Ron Wyden (D-OR), as well as Representatives Becca Balint (D-VT), Suzanne Bonamici (D-OR), Yvette D. Clarke (D-NY), Steve Cohen (D-TN), Jasmine Crockett (D-TX), Madeleine Dean (D-PA), Diana DeGette (D-CO), Mark DeSaulnier (D-CA), Maxine Dexter (D-OR), Veronica Escobar (D-TX), Maxwell Alejandro Frost (D-FL), Sylvia R. Garcia (D-TX), Chrissy Houlahan (D-PA), Val Hoyle (D-OR), Jonathan L. Jackson (D-IL), Pramila Jayapal (D-WA), Sydney Kamlager-Dove (D-CA), Sarah McBride (D-DE), Eleanor Holmes Norton (D-DC), Ilhan Omar (D-MN), Chellie Pingree (D-ME), Mike Quigley (D-IL), Emily Randall (D-WA), Jamie Raskin (D-MD), Mary Gay Scanlon (D-PA), Jan Schakowsky (D-IL), Greg Stanton (D-AZ), Dina Titus (D-NV), Rashida Tlaib (D-MI), James R. Walkinshaw (D-VA), Debbie Wasserman Schultz (D-FL), and Maxine Waters (D-CA).

Washington, DC – Today, U.S. Representatives Young Kim (CA-40) and Joyce Beatty (OH-03), Co-Chairs of the bipartisan Financial Literacy & Wealth Creation Caucus, introduced a resolution to designate October as National Financial Planning Month.

The resolution highlights the importance of improving financial education, expanding access to financial planning services, and empowering Americans to make more informed decisions that strengthen their long-term financial security.

“A paycheck only stretches so far when you’re juggling groceries, mortgage payments, and saving for the future,” said Rep. Young Kim. “Southern California families work hard for every dollar, and they deserve the tools to make their money go further and plan for what’s ahead. I’m proud to join Rep. Beatty in recognizing October as National Financial Planning Month and helping more Americans build the financial security they’ve worked so hard to achieve.”

“Financial security should not be a privilege reserved for the wealthy. Every American deserves the knowledge, resources, and tools to build a financially secure future,” said Rep. Joyce Beatty. “At a time when hardworking families are struggling with rising costs, saving for retirement, and making ends meet, financial literacy is more important than ever. I am proud to introduce this resolution recognizing October as National Financial Planning Month to highlight the importance of financial education. When we invest in financial literacy, we invest in stronger families, stronger communities, and a stronger economy.”

The resolution aims to increase national awareness of financial planning and encourage activities that strengthen financial literacy by:

Increasing public awareness about the importance of financial planning;

Expanding financial education in schools and colleges;

Promoting access to trusted financial planning services;

Helping Americans make informed decisions on budgeting, saving, investing, and retirement;

Supporting pro bono initiatives that provide no-cost financial guidance to underserved and economically vulnerable families.
